// FoldConstOperands — a Std->Std pass that moves a CONSTANT operand of a `binOp`
// or `cmp` into an IMMEDIATE, so the literal never occupies a register. It runs
// after `lowerMaxonToStd` and before the backend lowers Std to Target.
//
// The design ("§Phase 2, foldConstOperands") splits this from the const-folding pass `foldConstants`
// (evaluate `const <op> const`). ⚠ **THAT PASS WAS DEFERRED AND NOW EXISTS (EC12, 2026-08-28)** — it is
// scheduled immediately BEFORE this one, in `IR/Std/FoldConstants.maxon`. The deferral's stated reason
// was that folding every constant expression to a single `mov` would erase the codegen the spec
// fragments exist to show; goldens became REFERENCE rather than a gate on 2026-08-27 (`1152bbc566`), so
// that reason expired, and the specs whose SUBJECT is the emitted arithmetic were rewritten to take a
// runtime operand instead of a literal.
//
// The SPLIT itself is unchanged and is what the two passes still are. This one touches ONLY the operand
// FORM — a const stays materialized as a value; it just stops occupying a register at the op that reads
// it — so an expression with ONE live operand still lowers to real arithmetic, now `imul`/`lea` with the
// literal as an immediate instead of a register per literal. `foldConstants` handles the case where
// BOTH operands are constant, which is a different question with different traps (overflow, shift
// windows, and a division that must not be answered for at all).
//
// Four moves — the design's three, plus the IDENTITY fold that only becomes visible once move 2 has
// run:
//   1. Canonicalize a COMMUTATIVE op (`add`, `mul`) so the constant lands on the
//      rhs (swap when it started on the lhs).
//   2. Rewrite a const-rhs `binOp`/`cmp` into the `binOpImm`/`cmpImm` form. A
//      const-lhs COMPARISON swaps operands AND flips the predicate (`5 < a` becomes
//      `a > 5`). A const-lhs NON-COMMUTATIVE arith op (`5 - a`) is LEFT ALONE — it
//      is NOT `a - 5`, and rewriting it would silently miscompile.
//   3. Replace a `binOpImm` whose immediate is its opcode's IDENTITY ELEMENT — `x * 1`, `x + 0`,
//      `x - 0`, `x & -1`, `x | 0`, `x ^ 0`, `x shl/shr 0` — by its own operand: every read of the
//      result is rewritten to read the operand instead.
//   4. DCE the `const` op left with no remaining reference, and the identity op left with none.
//
// ⭐⭐ **WHY MOVE 3 IS HERE AND NOT IN A PASS OF ITS OWN.** It reads the form move 2 produces and it
// pays for nothing extra: the reference set move 4 already builds is exactly what decides whether the
// identity op may go, and the walk is the same walk. It is also where the shape is CREATED — move 2 is
// what turns `x * <const 1>` into the single op an identity test can recognise without a use count.
//
// ⚠ **IT IS NOT A GENERAL DCE AND MUST NOT GROW INTO ONE.** Move 4 retires exactly two kinds of def —
// a `const` and an identity `binOpImm` — because those are the two THIS pass detached from their
// readers. An unreferenced `call` is not dead (it may have run for its effects) and an unreferenced
// `div` is not either (`isPure: false` — it traps); deciding that is another pass's question.
//
// A const is folded only when it fits the immediate field of the lowered op — see
// `constFitsBinOpImmediate`, which is the ONE place that per-opcode rule lives. A
// constant outside it keeps its register operand, which is always a correct fallback.

// Each `const` result value id -> the constant it materializes. TWO DENSE COLUMNS, not a `Map`, and
// for exactly the reason `StdValueUseSet` states: a ValueId is already a dense, function-local index,
// so hashing one buys a cache miss and nothing else. This pass looks one up at every operand of every
// `binOp` and `cmp` in the program.
//
// EXPORTED, and read by TWO Std passes: this one (fold a const operand into an immediate) and
// `InsertRangeChecks` (decide whether a narrowing cast/return checks a compile-time literal). The two
// share the CONTAINER but not its FILL — each has its own populate walk (`collectConstValues` here,
// `collectConsts` there, which additionally folds a `neg` of a const). It is one type because the two
// were byte-identical: a second copy is a dense column whose `growFilled` trap and bounds logic nothing
// keeps in step with this one, which is the drift `StdValueUseSet` is a single exported type to avoid.
//
// (`ParsedIntArray` — the column of parsed integers — is declared in NumberParsing, beside the type
// it is a column of. The parser's constant folder keeps one too, over its own tier's value ids.)

export type ConstValueMap
	var isConst as BoolArray
	var constant as ParsedIntArray

	export static function create() returns ConstValueMap
		return Self{isConst: BoolArray.create(), constant: ParsedIntArray.create()}
	end 'create'

	// Record that `valueId` is a `const` materializing `value`. Both columns are extended with
	// `growFilled`, which grows their capacity GEOMETRICALLY — growing them to fit with `resize` (which
	// reserves exactly the length it is given) would be quadratic in a column extended one id at a time,
	// which is what a walk over the ops in order does. See `StdValueUseSet.insert`, the same column and
	// the same trap.
	export function upsert(valueId ValueId, value ParsedInt)
		self.isConst.growFilled(valueId + 1, value: false)
		self.constant.growFilled(valueId + 1, value: 0)

		try self.isConst.set(valueId, value: true) otherwise panic("ConstValueMap.upsert: isConst.set OOB at {valueId}")
		try self.constant.set(valueId, value: value) otherwise panic("ConstValueMap.upsert: constant.set OOB at {valueId}")
	end 'upsert'

	export function contains(valueId ValueId) returns bool
		if valueId >= self.isConst.count() 'notAConst'
			return false
		end 'notAConst'
		return try self.isConst.get(valueId) otherwise panic("ConstValueMap.contains: isConst.get OOB at {valueId}")
	end 'contains'

	// The constant `valueId` materializes. Panics for a value that is not a `const` — every caller has
	// already established that with `contains`, so there is no sentinel to return and none to mistake
	// for a real constant (0 is one).
	export function constantOf(valueId ValueId) returns ParsedInt
		if not self.contains(valueId) 'notAConst'
			panic("ConstValueMap.constantOf: value {valueId} is not a `const` — check `contains` first")
		end 'notAConst'
		return try self.constant.get(valueId) otherwise panic("ConstValueMap.constantOf: constant.get OOB at {valueId}")
	end 'constantOf'
end 'ConstValueMap'

// ⭐⭐ **WHAT ONE OP IS TO THIS PASS — WRITTEN INTO A CALLER-OWNED RECORD, NEVER RETURNED AS A UNION.**
//
// A PAYLOAD-BEARING UNION HEAP-BOXES EVERY CASE, PAYLOAD-FREE ONES INCLUDED — the finding EC1 measured
// in `InlineManagedPrimitives` (`30dc6c90b9`) — and this classification sits on a path walked ONCE PER
// OP, three times over: the const collect, the identity collect, and the prune. Returned as a union it
// minted and dropped a box for every op in the module that is neither a `const` nor an identity, which
// is almost all of them. It was `StdConstInfo` and `StdIdentityInfo`, two unions and two exhaustive
// `StdOp` rosters; it is now ONE roster filling this record in place, created once per walk.
//
// ⭐ **ONE ROSTER, NOT TWO, AND THAT IS THE POINT RATHER THAN A SIDE EFFECT.** The prune asks BOTH
// questions of the same op (`retiredDefs` was "unreferenced const OR unreferenced identity", two
// matches over one union), so the two classifications were already taken together at the one call site
// that needed both. Merged, a new `StdOp` variant is a compile error in ONE place instead of two, and
// the two answers cannot disagree about which band a variant falls in.
//
// It is the shape `collectStdOpUses` uses for the dual question — one exhaustive roster, a caller-owned
// sink, no allocation per op.
enum StdFoldableDefKind
	// Neither a `const` nor an identity `binOpImm`: nothing this pass detaches from a reader or retires.
	neither
	// A `const` — `definedId` is its result and `constant` the value it materializes.
	constDef
	// An identity `binOpImm` — `definedId` is its result and `identityOperand` the operand it computes.
	identityDef
end 'StdFoldableDefKind'

export type StdFoldableDef
	// ⚠ **THE THREE FIELDS BELOW ARE MEANINGLESS UNTIL `kind` SAYS WHICH OF THEM THE LAST
	// classification FILLED** — the discipline the union enforced by construction, and the one thing a
	// record has to state rather than enforce. Every reader asks a `kind` predicate first, and none of
	// them may be read as a sentinel: ValueId 0 and constant 0 are both REAL values here
	// (`DefinedValueLookup`'s header carries the incident that taught this compiler so).
	var kind as StdFoldableDefKind
	// The value the classified op defines — its result. Set by BOTH retirable kinds, which is what lets
	// the prune ask one question of either.
	export var definedId as ValueId
	// `constDef` only: the constant the `const` op materializes.
	export var constant as ParsedInt
	// `identityDef` only: the operand the identity `binOpImm` computes, and so the value every read of
	// `definedId` may read instead.
	export var identityOperand as ValueId

	export static function create() returns StdFoldableDef
		return Self{kind: StdFoldableDefKind.neither, definedId: 0, constant: 0, identityOperand: 0}
	end 'create'

	function recordNeither()
		self.kind = StdFoldableDefKind.neither
	end 'recordNeither'

	function recordConst(resultId ValueId, value ParsedInt)
		self.kind = StdFoldableDefKind.constDef
		self.definedId = resultId
		self.constant = value
	end 'recordConst'

	// A `binOpImm` is an IDENTITY only when its immediate is its opcode's identity element. The test
	// lives here rather than in the roster's arm so that arm carries the dispatch fact and nothing else.
	function recordBinOpImm(resultId ValueId, lhs ValueId, imm Imm32, opcode StdBinOpcode)
		if not binOpImmIsIdentity(opcode, imm: imm) 'notIdentity'
			self.recordNeither()
			return
		end 'notIdentity'

		self.kind = StdFoldableDefKind.identityDef
		self.definedId = resultId
		self.identityOperand = lhs
	end 'recordBinOpImm'

	export function isConstDef() returns bool
		return self.kind == StdFoldableDefKind.constDef
	end 'isConstDef'

	export function isIdentityDef() returns bool
		return self.kind == StdFoldableDefKind.identityDef
	end 'isIdentityDef'

	// A def this pass may retire — either kind. The prune's question, asked of `kind` rather than of the
	// op a second time.
	export function isRetirableDef() returns bool
		return self.kind != StdFoldableDefKind.neither
	end 'isRetirableDef'
end 'StdFoldableDef'

// Fill `def` with what `op` is to this pass. Exhaustive over StdOp, no bare `default`.
//
// ⭐ **ONLY `binOpImm` CAN BE AN IDENTITY, BY CONSTRUCTION — which is why the identity half needs one
// arm where the const half needs fifteen commented bands.** An identity is a fact about an op's
// IMMEDIATE OPERAND, and `binOpImm` is the only variant in the dialect that carries an immediate the
// arithmetic reads. (`cmpImm` carries one too, but it produces an i1 verdict rather than its operand,
// so no immediate makes it the identity.) A `binOp` whose rhs is a constant cannot be one either: by
// the time the identity collect runs, `rewriteBinAndCmpOps` has already turned every foldable const rhs
// into a `binOpImm`, and each of the three identity constants (0, 1, -1) fits every immediate field
// `constFitsBinOpImmediate` guards.
//
// ⚠ The bands are pinned to the union's FIRST and LAST variants, so a variant APPENDED at the end (the
// rule `StdOp`'s header states) falls outside all of them and is a COMPILE ERROR here rather than a
// silently-missed classification.
export function classifyFoldableDef(op StdOp, def StdFoldableDef)
	match op 'c'
		const(resultId, value, _) then def.recordConst(resultId, value: value)
		binOp to cmp then def.recordNeither()
		binOpImm(resultId, lhs, imm, opcode, _) then def.recordBinOpImm(resultId, lhs: lhs, imm: imm, opcode: opcode)
		// ⚠ Extended past `retVoid` to take in the memory band, and `loadIndirect` is the arm that
		// matters: a load's result is NOT a constant, however constant the global's INITIAL value in
		// the `.data` image is. That image is the value the program STARTS with, and any store since
		// has replaced it. Folding a load into its initializer would be the wrong answer
		// `loadIndirect`'s `isPure: false` DESCRIBES — `var counter = 0` … `counter = 42` … `return
		// counter` would return 0 — reached by a different road.
		//
		// ⚠ **"describes", not "prevents": `StdOpMeta.isPure` has ZERO readers** (see OPEN.md #27), so
		// it stops nothing, here or anywhere. **THIS BAND IS THE ONLY THING STANDING BETWEEN THAT WRONG
		// ANSWER AND THE PROGRAM** — it is a real check with a real reader (`classifyFoldableDef`), and
		// the purity flag beside it is a declaration awaiting its own. Do not read the flag's existence
		// as cover for narrowing this band.
		//
		// Extended past `storeIndirect` to take in the system band and the `osWriteStdout`/`rdataAddr`
		// tail. None is a constant a fold could read: an allocation's result is an OS-chosen address,
		// `osWriteStdout` defines no value, and `rdataAddr`'s result is a RIP-relative address, not a
		// constant `constValueOfDef` could rebuild. Extended past `rdataAddr` to the P1.4b tail: a
		// `tryCall`'s result is a call return, `errorReturn` defines nothing, `osExit` defines nothing.
		// Extended past `osExit` to the P1.5-A1 tail: `funcAddr`'s result is a RIP-relative `.text`
		// address — not a constant `constValueOfDef` could rebuild, exactly like `rdataAddr` — and
		// `callIndirect`'s result is a call return, like `call`/`tryCall`.
		cmpImm to osFreePages then def.recordNeither()
		// The jump table is a terminator — it defines no value, so nothing to fold a constant into.
		switch then def.recordNeither()
		// A witness call's result is a call return — not a constant this pass can rebuild, like `callIndirect`.
		witnessCall then def.recordNeither()
		witnessTryCall then def.recordNeither()
		// P1.5 dogfood slice 1a: an IOCP op's result is a kernel handle/BOOL/error, never a compile-time constant.
		osPipeCreate to osWriteFile then def.recordNeither()
		// The wall-clock read defines no value at all, so there is nothing to call constant.
		osReadWallClock then def.recordNeither()
		// R4.1 + R4.2: an OS file HANDLE, a Win32 BOOL and a search handle are kernel values, never
		// compile-time constants; the two void ops define nothing to call constant.
		osFileOpenRead to osFileAttributes then def.recordNeither()
		// A3n: the RENDERED panic reads a message pointer; in every other respect it is `osPanic`.
		osPanicValue then def.recordNeither()
		osCommandLine then def.recordNeither()
		// W5: a kernel HANDLE and the character count `GetModuleFileNameA` wrote are OS answers, never
		// compile-time constants.
		osStdHandle then def.recordNeither()
		osExecutablePath then def.recordNeither()
		// W35: the byte count the OS reports for a stderr write is an OS answer, exactly as its stdout
		// twin's is.
		osWriteStderr then def.recordNeither()
		// A frame address is a constant of the FRAME, not of the program: its value is not known until the
		// allocator has sized the slot area, so it is no more foldable than a global's address.
		stackRecordAddr then def.recordNeither()
		// O3: a section HANDLE, a mapped ADDRESS and a millisecond stopwatch reading are OS answers, never
		// compile-time constants.
		osOpenNamedSegment to osTickCountMs then def.recordNeither()
		// The rest of the appended band — the four attached-subprocess host primitives, the `ws2_32` socket
		// band, the machine query AND the thread-CPU read — are import calls too: a pipe's byte count, an
		// environment string, a SOCKET and a byte count the kernel chose are OS answers, never compile-time
		// constants.
		osHandleSetInherit to osThreadCpuTicks then def.recordNeither()
		// F1a: the decommit defines no value at all, so there is no def for a later use to fold a constant out
		// of — `recordNeither` is the only answer available, as it is for every result-less op here.
		osDecommitPages then def.recordNeither()
		// F1b: a fill defines no value either — its own fill BYTE is already a compile-time constant on the op,
		// which is a different thing from a def a later use could fold.
		memFill then def.recordNeither()
		// F1c: both DO define a value, and it is an address the OS chose at RUN time — never a compile-time
		// constant, exactly as `osAllocPages`'s page base is not.
		osCommitPages to osReservePages then def.recordNeither()
		// F2 + G1: every value-defining member of this range answers something read out of memory or out of
		// the machine at RUN time — a CAS's success boolean, an RMW's prior word, an OS thread's identity — so
		// none is a compile-time constant.
		atomicCas to osEnterBackgroundPriority then def.recordNeither()
		osFileOpenWriteExecutable then def.recordNeither()
		osEnvBlock to osEnvBlockFree then def.recordNeither()
		// G3: an ordered load answers a word another OS THREAD wrote, which is the opposite of a
		// compile-time constant; an ordered store defines nothing.
		loadAcquire or storeRelease then def.recordNeither()
	end 'c'
end 'classifyFoldableDef'

// Fold constant operands in `module.functions[firstFuncIndex …]`, in place.
//
// ⭐⭐ **IT TAKES A START INDEX BECAUSE IT RUNS TWICE, OVER TWO DISJOINT HALVES OF ONE MODULE (EC3-H).**
// The pipeline runs it over the whole module (`firstFuncIndex: 0`) — every function lowered from source.
// `Compiler.compileToCodeResult` then runs it a SECOND time over just the functions the runtime installs
// and the body synthesizers APPENDED after the pipeline, which the first run could not have seen because
// they did not exist yet. A start index is what makes the second run cost only what it folds; re-walking
// from 0 would re-collect a `ConstValueMap` for every user function to fold nothing in it.
//
// The two runs are disjoint by construction — the appended band starts exactly where the pipeline's module
// ended — so no function is folded twice, and folding is not idempotent-by-luck: it is simply never asked.
export function foldConstOperands(module StdModule, firstFuncIndex FuncIndex)
	logDebug(LogCategory.ir, message: function() gives "foldConstOperands: functions [{firstFuncIndex}, {module.funcCount()})")
	// An INDEX loop, not `for … in`: the element is a BORROW of `module`, and the call below is
	// recorded as writing that same storage. ⚖ Ruling of 2026-08-26 — canonical note at
	// `IR/Std/ElimTrivialBlockArgs.elimTrivialBlockArgs`.
	for fnIndex in firstFuncIndex upto module.functions.count() 'eachFunc'
		foldConstOperandsInFunc(module, func: try module.functions.get(fnIndex) otherwise panic("foldConstOperands: fnIndex {fnIndex} is below module.functions.count()"))
	end 'eachFunc'
end 'foldConstOperands'

// ⭐ **ONE `liveOpIndices` FOR THE WHOLE PASS OVER THIS FUNCTION, NOT ONE PER WALK.** Every step below
// visits exactly the same live ops, and none of them changes WHICH ops are live: the two rewrites replace
// an op IN PLACE at its own index (`module.ops.set`), and the only step that touches `block.opRefs` is
// `dceRetiredDefs`, which does it AFTER the last read of this column. So the column is built once and
// read four times.
//
// It is not a micro-optimization: `liveOpIndices` allocates a fresh `OpIndexArray` and `push`es one
// entry per live op into it, so a per-walk rebuild is a per-walk O(ops) allocation and copy. Four walks
// meant four of them (five once the identity substitution fired), and the pass runs over every function
// in the module.
function foldConstOperandsInFunc(module StdModule, func IrFunction)
	let opIndices = module.liveOpIndices(func)
	// ONE classification record for all three walks below — refilled per op, never re-created. See
	// `StdFoldableDef`'s header for why it is a record and not a returned union.
	var def = StdFoldableDef.create()

	let constMap = collectConstValues(module, opIndices: opIndices, def: def)
	rewriteBinAndCmpOps(module, opIndices: opIndices, constMap: constMap)
	// AFTER the immediate rewrite, which is what MAKES the identity shape, and BEFORE the prune,
	// which is what retires both kinds of def this pass detaches.
	substituteIdentityImmediates(module, func: func, opIndices: opIndices, def: def)
	dceRetiredDefs(module, func: func, opIndices: opIndices, def: def)
end 'foldConstOperandsInFunc'

// Map each `const` result value id to its constant. Value ids are function-local, so
// the map is rebuilt per function.
// ⚠ `export` SINCE EC18: `strengthReduceDivision` needs the same map, of the same function, for the
// same reason — its subject is a `div`/`mod` whose DIVISOR is a `const`. A second collector would be a
// second answer to "which values in this function are constants", over the same shared
// `classifyFoldableDef` roster, and the two would be free to drift about which ops count.
export function collectConstValues(module StdModule, opIndices OpIndexArray, def StdFoldableDef) returns ConstValueMap
	var constMap = ConstValueMap.create()
	for opIdx in opIndices 'eachOp'
		let op = try module.ops.get(opIdx) otherwise panic("foldConstOperands: live op index {opIdx} not in module.ops")
		classifyFoldableDef(op, def: def)
		if def.isConstDef() 'isConst'
			constMap.upsert(def.definedId, value: def.constant)
		end 'isConst'
	end 'eachOp'
	return constMap
end 'collectConstValues'

// Rewrite each `binOp`/`cmp` with a foldable constant operand into its immediate
// form, in place at the same op index (so block `opRefs`/terminators stay valid).
//
// The store is UNCONDITIONAL because `tryFold` hands back the op that belongs at the index — the
// ORIGINAL one where nothing folded — so an op that does not fold is written back over itself, which
// is a refcount and no allocation. It used to answer with a `FoldResult` union so this loop could skip
// the store, and that union was a heap box per op: the skip cost more than the store it saved.
// `substituteValuesInFunc` writes every op back for the same reason.
function rewriteBinAndCmpOps(module StdModule, opIndices OpIndexArray, constMap ConstValueMap)
	// ONE classification record for the whole walk, refilled per op and never re-created — see
	// `StdArithOperands`' header for why the answer is written into a caller-owned record rather than
	// returned as a union.
	var operands = StdArithOperands.create()

	for opIdx in opIndices 'eachOp'
		let op = try module.ops.get(opIdx) otherwise panic("foldConstOperands: live op index {opIdx} not in module.ops")
		try module.ops.set(opIdx, value: tryFold(op, constMap: constMap, operands: operands)) otherwise panic("foldConstOperands: ops.set OOB at {opIdx}")
	end 'eachOp'
end 'rewriteBinAndCmpOps'

// Rewrite every read of an IDENTITY `binOpImm`'s result to read that op's own operand instead. The op
// itself is left where it is; `dceRetiredDefs` below is what removes it, and only once nothing reads
// it any more.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE PRUNE IS REFERENCE-DRIVEN, AND THAT IS WHAT MAKES THIS SAFE WITHOUT A CHAIN RESOLVER.**
// Identities chain — `(x * 1) + 0` is two of them, and the corpus produces exactly that pair (an enum
// ordinal's `(v == k) * 1` summed into an accumulator seeded at 0). A chain is resolved HERE, at record
// time, by reading the column that is being built: `x * 1` is recorded first, so `+ 0`'s operand
// resolves through it to `x` in one step and both ops go.
//
// That works because `liveOpIndices` walks blocks in `func.blockRefs` order, which for a
// parser-generated CFG puts a def before its uses. It is not GUARANTEED to — `blockRefs` is LAYOUT
// order, not dominance order (`IrModule.blockIndexById`) — and the prune is why that costs nothing but
// a missed fold: an entry left pointing at a value that folds LATER leaves that value still READ, so
// its op is kept and the `imul`/`lea` survives. There is no arrangement in which a read is rewritten to
// a def that was deleted, which is the only failure that would matter.
//
// (`elimTrivialBlockArgs` DOES carry a union-find for its chains. It has to: its chains are built by a
// WORKLIST, in trigger order, so "record time" there has no order to lean on at all.)
function substituteIdentityImmediates(module StdModule, func IrFunction, opIndices OpIndexArray, def StdFoldableDef)
	var subst = ValueIdArray.create()

	for opIdx in opIndices 'eachOp'
		let op = try module.ops.get(opIdx) otherwise panic("foldConstOperands: live op index {opIdx} not in module.ops")
		classifyFoldableDef(op, def: def)
		if def.isIdentityDef() 'isIdentity'
			recordValueSubstitution(subst, resultId: def.definedId, replacement: def.identityOperand)
		end 'isIdentity'
	end 'eachOp'

	// An EMPTY column means no identity was found — `recordValueSubstitution` always extends it by at
	// least the one slot it writes — so a function without one pays neither the rewrite walk nor the
	// branch-edge rebuild. Most functions are in that case.
	if subst.count() == 0 'nothingToRewrite'
		return
	end 'nothingToRewrite'

	substituteValuesInFunc(module, func: func, subst: subst)
end 'substituteIdentityImmediates'

// THE per-opcode identity rule — `x <opcode> imm == x` — and the one place it lives, exactly as
// `constFitsBinOpImmediate` below is the one place the per-opcode immediate RANGE lives.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE ANSWER DOES NOT DEPEND ON `operandType`, BECAUSE EVERY IMMEDIATE FORM COMPUTES AT THE
// MACHINE WORD ON ALL THREE TARGETS.** x64's `lowerBinOpImm` selects `lea`/`imul`/`and`/`or`/`xor` over
// 64-bit registers and reads no width at all; arm64's selects the X-register forms; wasm's
// `emitBinOpImm` computes at `wasmMachineValTypeOf`, which is `i64` for every integer type. So a narrow
// `operandType` is a STORAGE class here and never an arithmetic one, and `x + 0` at i8 is the same 64
// bits as `x` — which is what makes substituting the operand for the result exact rather than merely
// equal-in-range. (`operandType` still TRAVELS with the immediate fold, for the reason `tryFoldBinOp`
// gives: a rewrite that KEEPS an op must not change the width it asks about. This one keeps none.)
//
// ⚠ **THERE IS NO FLOAT ARM AND NONE WOULD BE CORRECT.** `x * 1.0` is not `x` — it quiets a signalling
// NaN — and no float `binOpImm` exists to classify anyway, because `tryFoldBinOp` refuses a float
// operand type (no SSE/NEON/wasm form takes an immediate). The float-only opcodes answer `false` for
// the same reason `constFitsBinOpImmediate`'s arm does: it is the answer that stays correct if the
// arm ever stops being unreachable.
function binOpImmIsIdentity(opcode StdBinOpcode, imm Imm32) returns bool
	return match opcode 'o'
		add gives imm == AdditiveIdentity
		sub gives imm == AdditiveIdentity
		mul gives imm == MultiplicativeIdentity
		bitAnd gives imm == AllBitsSet
		bitOr to bitXor gives imm == AdditiveIdentity
		shl to shrLogical gives imm == NoShift
		div to max gives false
		// EC18's high multiply HAS no immediate form (`constFitsBinOpImmediate` refuses every constant
		// for it), so no `binOpImm(mulHighSigned, …)` is ever produced and this arm is unreachable.
		// `false` is nevertheless the right answer rather than a placeholder: `mulhi(x, 1)` is 0 for
		// every non-negative x and -1 for every negative one — it is not `x`, so there is no immediate
		// this operation is the identity at.
		mulHighSigned gives false
	end 'o'
end 'binOpImmIsIdentity'

// The identity ELEMENTS, named because each is a fact about an OPERATION rather than a number that
// happens to appear here.
//
// `bitOr`/`bitXor` share the additive one and that is not a coincidence of value: an all-zero operand
// sets no bit and flips none, which is the same statement about a bit that `+ 0` is about a sum.
let AdditiveIdentity = 0 as Imm32
let MultiplicativeIdentity = 1 as Imm32

// ⚠ `-1`, NOT `0xFFFFFFFF`. The immediate field is SIGNED and every target sign-extends it to the
// machine word, so `-1` is an all-ones 64-bit mask while `4294967295` would be a 32-bit TRUNCATION —
// a different question, and one this fold must never answer yes to. (It cannot arrive here anyway:
// `fitsImm32` rejects it, so a real u32 mask keeps its register operand.)
let AllBitsSet = -1 as Imm32

// A shift of zero moves no bit, at every width and in both directions.
let NoShift = 0 as Imm32

// Attempt to fold one op, answering with THE OP THAT BELONGS AT ITS INDEX — the folded immediate
// form, or `op` itself where nothing folds. Never a union: "unchanged" spelled as a `FoldResult` case
// cost a heap box on a path walked once per op, and the op is a truthful answer that costs nothing
// (the same cure, for the same reason, as `StdFoldableDef`'s).
//
// ⭐ **WHICH OPS ARE SUBJECTS AT ALL IS `classifyArithOperands`' ANSWER, NOT THIS FUNCTION'S.** This
// used to open with its own exhaustive `StdOp` match whose every arm but two returned `op` — the same
// enumeration `foldConstants` would then have needed a SECOND copy of, in a second file that would
// learn about a new variant separately. The roster is now the dialect's, stated once, and it is where
// the reason each excluded band is excluded now lives: `div`/`mod` TRAP, the immediate forms carry no
// second value operand, and the rest compute nothing an immediate could be folded into.
//
// ⚠ **ONE EXCLUSION IS THIS PASS'S OWN AND IS DELIBERATELY NOT IN THAT ROSTER: A STORE'S VALUE.** It
// is the tempting one — `counter = 42` really does store a constant, and x64 really does have
// `mov [mem], imm32`. Taking it would mean `storeIndirect` growing an immediate twin
// (`storeIndirectImm`) with its own operand model, splitter and colorer arms, to save one `mov` per
// constant store. Priced and not taken — the same call `binOpImm` made in reverse, where the win was
// every literal in every expression rather than the initializing store of a global.
function tryFold(op StdOp, constMap ConstValueMap, operands StdArithOperands) returns StdOp
	classifyArithOperands(op, operands: operands)

	if operands.isBinaryArith() 'arith'
		return tryFoldBinOp(op, resultId: operands.resultId, lhs: operands.lhs, rhs: operands.rhs, opcode: operands.opcode, operandType: operands.operandType, constMap: constMap)
	end 'arith'

	if operands.isComparison() 'comparison'
		return tryFoldCmp(op, resultId: operands.resultId, lhs: operands.lhs, rhs: operands.rhs, pred: operands.pred, operandType: operands.operandType, constMap: constMap)
	end 'comparison'

	return op
end 'tryFold'

// `resultId = lhs <opcode> rhs`. A const rhs folds directly. For a COMMUTATIVE op a
// const lhs is swapped onto the rhs (`k + a` becomes `a + k`). A non-commutative op
// with a const lhs and non-const rhs (`5 - a`, `1 shl n`… where `n` is not const) is
// left alone — it is not `a - 5` / `n shl 1`.
//
// ⚠ A FLOAT NEVER FOLDS: there is no SSE instruction that takes an immediate operand,
// so there is no float form to fold INTO. Without the guard the operands' IEEE bit
// patterns would ride an INTEGER `add reg, imm32` / `imul` — a silent wrong answer.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE OPERAND TYPE TRAVELS WITH THE FOLD (X5).** It used to be dropped here, on the
// reading that "no float can reach the immediate form" was the whole of what the type
// said. It is not: the type also says how WIDE the operation is, and a target without
// 64-bit registers everywhere had to re-derive that from `lhs` — which a fold does not
// promise agrees (`a mod b`'s overflow mask adds `-1` to an i1 at i64 width). A rewrite
// that changes the INSTRUCTION must not change the QUESTION; carrying the type is how it
// does not.
function tryFoldBinOp(op StdOp, resultId ValueId, lhs ValueId, rhs ValueId, opcode StdBinOpcode, operandType StdType, constMap ConstValueMap) returns StdOp
	if stdTypeIsFloat(operandType) 'float'
		return op
	end 'float'
	if foldableBinOpImm(constMap, id: rhs, opcode: opcode) 'rhsConst'
		return StdOp.binOpImm(resultId, lhs: lhs, imm: constAsImmediate(constMap, id: rhs), opcode: opcode, operandType: operandType)
	end 'rhsConst'
	if binOpcodeIsCommutative(opcode) and foldableBinOpImm(constMap, id: lhs, opcode: opcode) 'lhsConst'
		return StdOp.binOpImm(resultId, lhs: rhs, imm: constAsImmediate(constMap, id: lhs), opcode: opcode, operandType: operandType)
	end 'lhsConst'
	return op
end 'tryFoldBinOp'

// `resultId = lhs <pred> rhs`. A const rhs folds directly. A const lhs swaps operands
// AND flips the predicate (`k < a` == `a > k`), which every comparison supports (no
// silent-miscompile case — unlike arith `sub`). `cmpRegImm32` carries the immediate
// verbatim in a signed 32-bit field, so the whole Imm32 domain folds.
//
// ⚠⚠ A FLOAT NEVER FOLDS — and this is the guard most likely to be believed unnecessary,
// because removing it PASSES the obvious test. `ucomisd` has no immediate form, so a
// folded float compare becomes an integer `cmp reg, imm32` over IEEE bit patterns. For
// two POSITIVE doubles that still answers correctly — sign-magnitude bit patterns happen
// to order the same way as the values — so `3.5 > 9.5` is green and the bug hides. It
// surfaces only on a negative operand (whose sign bit inverts the integer order) or a
// NaN (which no integer compare can report as unordered).
// specs-shv2/float-compare-branch.md `lt-nan-is-false` is the case that catches it.
function tryFoldCmp(op StdOp, resultId ValueId, lhs ValueId, rhs ValueId, pred StdCmpPred, operandType StdType, constMap ConstValueMap) returns StdOp
	if stdTypeIsFloat(operandType) 'float'
		return op
	end 'float'
	if foldableCmpImm(constMap, id: rhs) 'rhsConst'
		return StdOp.cmpImm(resultId, lhs: lhs, imm: constAsImmediate(constMap, id: rhs), pred: pred, operandType: operandType)
	end 'rhsConst'
	if foldableCmpImm(constMap, id: lhs) 'lhsConst'
		return StdOp.cmpImm(resultId, lhs: rhs, imm: constAsImmediate(constMap, id: lhs), pred: flipCmpPred(pred), operandType: operandType)
	end 'lhsConst'
	return op
end 'tryFoldCmp'

// Flip a comparison predicate for an operand swap: `a <pred> b` == `b <flip(pred)> a`.
function flipCmpPred(pred StdCmpPred) returns StdCmpPred
	return match pred 'p'
		eq gives StdCmpPred.eq
		ne gives StdCmpPred.ne
		lt gives StdCmpPred.gt
		gt gives StdCmpPred.lt
		le gives StdCmpPred.ge
		ge gives StdCmpPred.le
	end 'p'
end 'flipCmpPred'

// True iff `id` names a `const` whose value fits the immediate field of `opcode`'s
// lowered form. Not a `const` at all ⇒ false (nothing to fold).
function foldableBinOpImm(constMap ConstValueMap, id ValueId, opcode StdBinOpcode) returns bool
	if not constMap.contains(id) 'notConst'
		return false
	end 'notConst'

	return constFitsBinOpImmediate(constMap.constantOf(id), opcode: opcode)
end 'foldableBinOpImm'

// The comparison twin of `foldableBinOpImm`: `cmpRegImm32` carries the immediate verbatim
// in a signed 32-bit field, so the whole Imm32 domain folds regardless of the predicate.
function foldableCmpImm(constMap ConstValueMap, id ValueId) returns bool
	if not constMap.contains(id) 'notConst'
		return false
	end 'notConst'

	return fitsImm32(constMap.constantOf(id))
end 'foldableCmpImm'

// THE per-opcode immediate rule — the one place it lives, so the fold and the x64
// lowering it feeds cannot disagree about what an immediate form can carry.
//
//   add / mul / bitAnd / bitOr / bitXor — the immediate is encoded VERBATIM in a signed
//       32-bit field (`lea [base + imm]`, `imul r,r,imm32`, `<alu> r/m64, imm32`), so the
//       whole Imm32 domain folds.
//   sub — lowers to `lea [base - imm]`, a NEGATED displacement, and -(i32.min) overflows a
//       signed 32-bit field. i32.min alone is excluded.
//   shl / shr — the immediate is an imm8 SHIFT COUNT, so folding is restricted to the counts the
//       INSTRUCTION takes as written (TypeRules' `shiftCountIsUnguarded` — ASKED here rather than
//       restated). Those are exactly the counts on which the imm8 form and the `cl` form provably
//       agree, because the hardware's mask is a no-op on them.
//       A count outside it never reaches this pass as a constant, and the parser has THREE routes to
//       that — not two, which is what this paragraph said before EC6 added the third:
//         FOLDED  — the parser saw the value (`x shl 100` IS the constant 0), so no count above the
//                   window survives as a shift at all.
//         GUARDED — the parser saw neither the value nor a bound, so it saturated the count at run
//                   time (`Parser.emitGuardedShift`).
//         PROVEN  — the parser saw the count's declared BOUNDS and they lie inside the window
//                   (`Parser.shiftCountIsProvenUnguarded`), so a constant this pass discovers for it
//                   is one this window already covers.
//       ⚠ The three do NOT all leave the same fallback. If a count outside the window somehow reached
//       here it would keep its register operand and go through `cl`, and that is correct for a GUARDED
//       count because the guard the parser emitted saturates it first — a PROVEN count has no guard,
//       so for it the fallback is correct only because the bound it was proven against IS this window.
//       That is why both ask TypeRules' one predicate rather than restating it.
function constFitsBinOpImmediate(v ParsedInt, opcode StdBinOpcode) returns bool
	return match opcode 'o'
		add gives fitsImm32(v)
		mul gives fitsImm32(v)
		sub gives fitsImm32(v) and v != i32.min
		bitAnd to bitXor gives fitsImm32(v)
		shl to shrLogical gives shiftCountIsUnguarded(v)
		// The three FLOAT-ONLY opcodes — NO constant fits any of them, because no SSE/NEON/wasm form of
		// `divsd`/`minsd`/`maxsd` takes an encoded immediate at all. Unreachable (the float guard in
		// `tryFoldBinOp` returns first), and `false` is the answer that stays correct if it ever
		// stops being unreachable.
		div to max gives false
		// ⭐ EC18 — NO constant fits the high multiply, and this arm is LOAD-BEARING rather than
		// defensive. x64's one-operand `imul r/m64` has no immediate form at all, and the multiplier
		// this opcode exists to carry is a full 64-BIT magic number that an `Imm32` field could not
		// hold in the first place. Answering `true` for the magics that DO happen to fit 32 bits (every
		// divisor above 2^32 has one) would mint a `binOpImm(mulHighSigned, …)` that no backend lowers.
		mulHighSigned gives false
	end 'o'
end 'constFitsBinOpImmediate'

// True iff `v` fits the signed 32-bit immediate field every DIRECT-immediate x64 form
// carries.
function fitsImm32(v ParsedInt) returns bool
	return v >= i32.min and v <= i32.max
end 'fitsImm32'

// The constant of `id` as an Imm32 — the caller has already established it fits, via
// `foldableBinOpImm` / `foldableCmpImm`, so the cast cannot overflow. `constantOf` panics
// on a value that is not a `const` at all, so there is no sentinel to mistake for one.
function constAsImmediate(constMap ConstValueMap, id ValueId) returns Imm32
	return constMap.constantOf(id) as Imm32
end 'constAsImmediate'

// Drop every def this pass detached from its readers and that nothing else reads: the rhs `const`s the
// immediate rewrite took out of their `binOp`/`cmp`, and the identity `binOpImm`s whose reads
// `substituteIdentityImmediates` moved onto their own operand. Either one still feeding another op or a
// phi branch edge is kept.
//
// ⭐ **IT DECIDES WHAT DIES; `removeOpsDefining` PERFORMS THE REMOVAL.** The block-rebuild half is the
// dialect's, shared with `eliminateCommonSubexpressions`, which dooms an entirely different set for an
// entirely different reason and used to need a second copy of the identical walk. What stays here is
// the only part that is this pass's own — WHICH defs are retirable, which is `StdFoldableDef`'s two
// kinds and a reference test.
export function dceRetiredDefs(module StdModule, func IrFunction, opIndices OpIndexArray, def StdFoldableDef)
	removeOpsDefining(module, func: func, doomed: retiredDefs(module, func: func, opIndices: opIndices, def: def))
end 'dceRetiredDefs'

// The value ids the prune drops: a `const` or an identity `binOpImm` whose result appears in no
// reference set. ONE classification answers for both kinds — see `StdFoldableDef` — where it used to be
// two predicates over two unions, each with its own exhaustive `StdOp` roster.
//
// ⚠ The reference test is not a formality for the identity kind — it is the whole safety argument for
// a substitution that was applied in walk order. See `substituteIdentityImmediates`.
function retiredDefs(module StdModule, func IrFunction, opIndices OpIndexArray, def StdFoldableDef) returns StdValueUseSet
	// The reachability roots this prune keeps a def alive for. `collectFunctionValueUses` (StdDialect)
	// is the one statement of "every value this function reads" — it walks the same ops `opIndices`
	// names, and it is what keeps the branch-edge half from being forgotten at one of its two callers.
	var referenced = StdValueUseSet.create()
	collectFunctionValueUses(module, func: func, referenced: referenced)
	var doomed = StdValueUseSet.create()

	for opIdx in opIndices 'eachOp'
		let op = try module.ops.get(opIdx) otherwise panic("foldConstOperands: opIdx {opIdx} not in module.ops")
		classifyFoldableDef(op, def: def)

		if not def.isRetirableDef() 'notRetirable'
			continue
		end 'notRetirable'

		if referenced.contains(def.definedId) 'stillRead'
			continue
		end 'stillRead'

		doomed.insert(def.definedId)
	end 'eachOp'

	return doomed
end 'retiredDefs'
